ntdll: NtWriteVirtualMemory on the current process writes directly.

Wine writes another process's memory through wineserver, which on macOS writes through the Mach task port. macOS
refuses the server that port, so WriteProcessMemory failed with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED even when a process wrote its own
code. In-process hookers do exactly that: the Steam overlay (GameOverlayRenderer64.dll) logged "WriteProcessMemory()
call failed trying to overwrite first 5 bytes of function body during hook" for every function and never showed.
NtWriteVirtualMemory now writes the current process itself with virtual_uninterrupted_write_memory (write access
checked and write watches updated under the virtual lock, then one memcpy; no probing write into the target), as
NtReadVirtualMemory already reads it. An x64 test that rewrites the first five bytes of XInputGetState, GetCursorPos,
CreateDXGIFactory1 and Sleep through WriteProcessMemory got ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED before and succeeds after (M5 Max,
macOS 27.2, 2026-11-17). The overlay also needs 0064, and its swap chain hooks need DXVK's methods to be detourable
on ARM64EC, which is a separate change.

The memcpy can fault. With 0019, image pages a program made WRITECOPY stay read-execute on the host until the first
write, so the copy faults inside the uninterrupted section; the fault handler resolves it, which works only because
virtual_mutex is recursive and SIGSEGV is not blocked in that section. virtual_uninterrupted_write_memory's premise
that nothing faults inside it no longer holds for these pages.

Pages FEX keeps write-trapped while the program sees them writable (0043) are read-execute in Wine's view, so the
direct write refuses them with STATUS_PARTIAL_COPY, where Windows writes. kernelbase's WriteProcessMemory only makes
pages writable that read back as not writable, and with 0043 they read back as the program's read-write-execute, so
ntdll's ARM64EC NtWriteVirtualMemory now handles that case: when the write fails on the current process, each page
that is not writable on the host but writable for the program (the emulator's GuestProtection) gets the program's
protection back through NtProtectVirtualMemory, which tells the emulator to drop its translations of the page, and
the write is retried once. The emulator protects the page again when it next translates it.

This turns every program that patches itself through WriteProcessMemory, overlays, DRM, anti-cheat, mod loaders and
Forza Horizon 6's copy protection, from failing to succeeding. tests/x64-fidelity/wpmtest.c writes data, the program's
own image code, and generated code that has already run (read-write-execute and read-execute, through
WriteProcessMemory and NtWriteVirtualMemory), and calls the code after each write. M5 Max, macOS 27.2, 2026-11-19:
before 0063, the writes fail with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED; with the unix part alone 10/2 (both failures are the
write-trapped read-write-execute page, ERROR_PARTIAL_COPY and STATUS_PARTIAL_COPY); with the ARM64EC part 12/0.
memtest 53/0, smcdata and smcretire 0 stale calls, unchanged.
Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 was not tried: we do not have it.

Upstream: not sent.

